    (v_topic_id, 'A 48-old male with type 2 diabetes presents to the clinic for a routine follow-up. Lab tests reveal a serum creatinine of 1.8 mg/dL and an estimated glomerular filtration rate (eGFR) of 26 mL/min/1.73m². They are currently taking Metformin 1000 mg twice daily. Which of the following is the most appropriate management regarding their Metformin therapy?', '["Transition to intravenous Metformin to bypass first-pass hepatic metabolism", "Continue the current dose but advise the patient to avoid all dietary carbohydrates", "Reduce the Metformin dose by half and continue close monitoring of renal function", "Add a loop diuretic to enhance renal excretion of Metformin", "Discontinue Metformin immediately due to the high risk of drug accumulation and lactic acidosis"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Metformin is cleared renally by glomerular filtration and active tubular secretion. In patients with severe renal impairment (eGFR <30 mL/min/1.73m²), Metformin clearance is significantly reduced, leading to drug accumulation. This accumulation increases the risk of metformin-associated lactic acidosis (MALA), a rare but life-threatening complication characterized by elevated lactate and severe metabolic acidosis. Metformin is strictly contraindicated in patients with an eGFR <30 mL/min/1.73m² and should be discontinued immediately.'),
